//! image_report: hand a path to an image and get its real facts back, measured
//! by a Python worker rather than by the server.
//!
//! This is the counterpart to `examples/shell-run`. There, an action carried a
//! declared `run` body and the server executed it with no worker at all. Here
//! the action has NO body, so it is served the other way: a separate Python
//! process registers against the `python_box` queue and answers the dispatch.
//! The same AWL file cannot tell you which it will be — that is the point. An
//! action is a typed obligation; where it is discharged is an operational
//! choice, not a language one.
workflow image_report
  input path: String

  outcome inspected: type ImageFacts, route success

/// What the worker actually measured, read out of the file's own header rather
/// than guessed from its extension — a `.png` that is really a JPEG reports
/// JPEG, because the bytes are the authority and the filename is not.
type ImageFacts {
  path: String,
  format: String,
  width: Int,
  height: Int,
  bytes: Int,
}

/// No `run` body: a remote worker must serve this queue. The worker block's
/// name IS the task queue, so a worker registering under `python_box` with a
/// matching typed action surface is what makes this workflow runnable.
///
/// The return type being a declared record is load-bearing. The server checks a
/// worker's advertised output schema against this contract covariantly, so a
/// worker that shrugs and advertises "I return anything" is refused here. It
/// has to actually know what it produces.
worker python_box
  action inspect_image(path: String) -> ImageFacts

step inspect_it
  path |> inspect_image |> route inspected
